# This is the configuration surface for running the api and web processes
# natively, required by `make dev-local` (which loads it into the
# environment). It is NOT consumed by the Compose stack (`make dev` /
# `make up`): docker-compose.yml hardcodes every value inline, with no
# ${VAR} interpolation and no env_file:, so editing this file has no effect
# there. Copy it to .env before running `make dev-local`.
#
# DATABASE_URL points at localhost:5432, not postgres:5432, because with
# dev-local the api runs natively while Postgres runs in Docker.
APP_ENV=development
PORT=8080
DATABASE_URL=postgres://hearth:hearth@localhost:5432/hearth?sslmode=disable
SMTP_ADDR=localhost:1025
SMTP_FROM="Hearth <noreply@hearth.localhost>"
# SMTP_USERNAME and SMTP_PASSWORD are optional -- leave both unset (as here)
# for a relay with no authentication, such as the Mailpit container this
# Compose stack ships with. Set both together for a relay that requires
# authentication; one without the other is rejected at startup.
#SMTP_USERNAME=
#SMTP_PASSWORD=
# SMTP_TLS_MODE is "none", "opportunistic" or "mandatory". It defaults to
# "none" when APP_ENV=development (Mailpit speaks plain SMTP) and to
# "mandatory" for every other APP_ENV, since no hosted relay accepts an
# unauthenticated, unencrypted connection. Only set this explicitly to
# override that default.
#SMTP_TLS_MODE=
APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5173
ARGON2_TIME=3
ARGON2_MEMORY_KIB=65536
ARGON2_THREADS=2

# --- Production deployment note --------------------------------------------
# APP_ENV=production (or anything other than "development") makes the
# session and CSRF cookies Secure, meaning a browser will only ever return
# them over HTTPS. This service itself never terminates TLS -- it only
# listens on PORT over plain HTTP -- so TLS termination in front of it (a
# reverse proxy or load balancer) is mandatory outside development. Deployed
# without one, every cookie this service sets is silently dropped and every
# authenticated request 401s. The api process logs a warning at startup
# whenever APP_ENV != development as a reminder; see also web/nginx.conf,
# which listens on plain :80 for the identical reason (TLS termination is
# assumed to happen upstream of it, not inside it).
#
# APP_BASE_URL must be the public HTTPS origin in production, not a
# placeholder. Every magic link, invite and sign-up link is composed from it
# and mailed -- so a wrong value does not fail at startup or in any test, it
# ships URLs pointing at localhost to real people's inboxes, and magic link
# is the only way back into a locked-out household. It is also the origin the
# session and CSRF cookies are scoped to; see docs/HANDOVER.md section 5 on
# why the domain, not the server, is the fragile part of this deployment.
#
# Nothing is deployed yet, though the deployment itself is now written:
# deploy/docker-compose.prod.yml, deploy/Caddyfile and the scripts beside them
# are real, and api/Dockerfile's `admin` target puts goose and adminctl on the
# production surface -- so a deployed install CAN unlock a locked-out
# household, apply a migration and reset a password. deploy/README.md carries
# every one of those commands.
#
# docs/adr/0001-optimise-for-exit-cost.md and
# docs/adr/0002-first-production-host.md carry the hosting decision, and
# docs/HANDOVER.md section 4 lists what still stands between it and a live
# install: a box, the DNS and mail records, a scheduled backup with an
# exercised escrow, and a browser walk against the deployed install.
#
# This file is the DEVELOPMENT template. Production has its own,
# deploy/.env.example, whose values differ -- most sharply on SMTP_USERNAME
# and SMTP_PASSWORD, which must both be filled or both left blank there.
